<section class="non-normative">
## Introduction ## {#introduction}

*This section is non-normative.*

A WebExtension can declare a [=/service worker=] as its
<a href="https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-background">background</a>
context. Browsers make some capabilities available in that worker that are
not available in other [=/service workers=]. This is done where user
permission is better granted at a different level in the extension permission
model than the web offers, such as hardware access.

Today each such capability is implemented ad hoc, without specification,
because WebIDL has no way to name "the [=/service worker=] that belongs to an
extension". The result is that the IDL of the owning specification does not
match what Chromium, at present the only engine that ships these capabilities
in [=extension service workers=], implements:

<div class="example" id="example-status-quo">
<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>API</th>
      <th>The IDL says</th>
      <th>Chromium ships</th>
      <th>The gap</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>WebUSB [[WEBUSB]]</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=(Worker,Window)]`: every worker, including every
          [=/service worker=]. Added for [=extension service workers=] in
          <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/usb/pull/221">whatwg/usb#221</a>.</td>
      <td>Windows, dedicated workers, and the [=extension service worker=].</td>
      <td>The IDL claims shared workers and all [=/service workers=]. No
          engine implements that.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>WebHID [[WEBHID]]</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=(DedicatedWorker,ServiceWorker,Window)]`, with a note
          that a user agent "<span class=allow-2119>may</span>" limit the
          [=/service worker=] exposure to extension contexts. Added in
          <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/hid/pull/97">whatwg/hid#97</a>.</td>
      <td>Windows, dedicated workers, and the [=extension service worker=].</td>
      <td>The restriction is in a note, not in the IDL.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Clipboard [[CLIPBOARD-APIS]]</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=Window]`. [=/Service worker=] exposure for the web was
          declined in
          <a href="https://github.com/w3c/editing/issues/458">w3c/editing#458</a>.</td>
      <td>The [=extension service worker=], without user activation, gated
          on the extension's clipboard permissions.</td>
      <td>No IDL describes the shipped exposure.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>

This specification does not define the lifetime of an
[=extension service worker=], and it does not revisit the processing model of
[=/service workers=].

This specification defines [=extension service workers=]. To do so it:

1.  Defines the [=extension service worker realm=], so that WebIDL can
    expose a construct in [=extension service workers=] without exposing it
    in other [=/service workers=].
1.  Sets the criteria this group applies before a capability is exposed to
    [=extension service workers=]. New behavior belongs in the feature's own
    specification and in [[SERVICE-WORKERS]]. Exposure to
    [=extension service workers=] alone is for the cases where that has been
    considered and declined.

</section>

## Conformance of user agents ## {#conformance-of-user-agents}

The requirements in this specification are on user agents that implement
[[SERVICE-WORKERS]] and support WebExtensions. They are stated in
[[#exposure-condition]] and [[#security]].

The criteria in [[#exposing]] are applied by this group. They are not
conformance requirements on any specification or user agent.

## Extension service workers ## {#extension-service-workers}

A WebExtension can declare a [=/service worker=] in its
<a href="https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-background">manifest</a>
under `background.service_worker`. The user agent registers it by running
[=register the background service worker=] [[webextensions]]. The resulting
[=/service worker registration=] is the WebExtension's
[=background service worker registration=]. Its [=service worker
registration/scope url=] is the WebExtension's root URL.

The <dfn export lt="extension service worker|background service worker">extension
service worker</dfn> of a WebExtension is the [=active worker=] of that
WebExtension's [=background service worker registration=]. A WebExtension has
at most one active [=extension service worker=] at a time, and none if its
manifest has no `background.service_worker` entry. The extension service
worker is a [=privileged extension context=]. The [=url/origin=] of its
[=service worker/script url=] is the [=extension origin=].

Note: The definition is in terms of the registration rather than a particular
[=/service worker=] because the [=/service worker=] is replaced when the
extension updates, and the registration persists. Every [=/service worker=] of
the registration, including one that is still installing, has an
[=extension service worker realm=], because a worker's realm is created
before the worker becomes active.

<div class="issue">
<p>The manifest is the only expected way to register an
[=extension service worker=]. A document on the [=extension origin=] can
nevertheless call {{ServiceWorkerContainer/register()}} with the
[=extension root URL=] as the scope. Within an origin, [[SERVICE-WORKERS]]
identifies a [=/service worker registration=] by its
[=service worker registration/scope url=], so such a call names the
[=background service worker registration=] and would replace its
[=/service worker=] with the script passed to the call.</p>

<p>This group needs to decide two things: whether such a registration is
permitted at all, and, if it is, whether the resulting [=/service worker=] is
the [=extension service worker=] under the definition above.</p>
</div>

An [=extension service worker=] is a [=secure context=], as
[[SERVICE-WORKERS]] requires, because [[webextensions]] makes each
[=extension scheme=] a scheme the user agent considers to be authenticated in
the sense of [[SECURE-CONTEXTS]].

## The extension service worker realm ## {#exposure-condition}

A [=realm=] is an <dfn export>extension service worker realm</dfn> if it was
created for a [=/service worker=] of a
[=background service worker registration=].

Note: An [=extension service worker realm=] is defined by the registration
the realm was created for, rather than by the realm's eventual
[=global object=], because WebIDL decides exposure while the realm's initial
objects are being created, before [[SERVICE-WORKERS]] associates the
[=global object=] with its [=/service worker=].

The [=extension service worker realm=] is meant to be used as a condition
on the `ServiceWorker` entry of a construct's [=exposure set=] in the
[{{Exposed}}] extended attribute. The construct is then [=exposed=] in the
[=extension service worker realm=] and not in other [=/service workers=].
Entries for other [=global names=] are unaffected. A construct whose
[=exposure set=] names `Worker`, which covers every worker global, lists
`DedicatedWorker` and `ServiceWorker` separately so that the condition can be
placed on the latter alone.

A user agent must not treat the presence of a construct in a [=realm=] as
authorization to use the capability it provides. The extension's permissions
are checked on each use, independently of exposure, as described in
[[#security]].

Issue: WebIDL does not currently allow a condition on an individual entry of
an [=exposure set=]. Its exposure conditions ([{{SecureContext}}] and
[{{CrossOriginIsolated}}]) apply to a whole construct. This requires a
proposal to WebIDL. Until WebIDL accepts a mechanism, no specification can
reference the [=extension service worker realm=] from IDL, and
[[#current-exposures]] describes intended exposure.

## Exposing a capability to extension service workers ## {#exposing}

### Criteria ### {#criteria}

When a capability is wanted in [=extension service workers=], this group
works through the following options in order and stops at the first that
applies.

1.  <dfn lt="exposure to all service workers">Expose it to all
    [=/service workers=]</dfn>. The group asks the specification that owns
    the construct to add `ServiceWorker` (or `Worker`) to its
    [=exposure set=]. If the feature needs the worker to be started, kept
    alive, or notified, the owning specification uses the extension points
    of [[SERVICE-WORKERS]]: a [=functional event=], an event handler on
    {{ServiceWorkerGlobalScope}}, and [=Fire Functional Event=]. If those
    extension points are insufficient, the missing hook is proposed to
    [[SERVICE-WORKERS]].

    <div class="example" id="example-all-workers-first">
    The `CSS` namespace is not [=exposed=] in workers. An extension wants
    `CSS.supports()` for feature detection in its
    [=extension service worker=]
    (<a href="https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1151">issue
    #1151</a>). The feature needs neither a document nor a prompt, so
    [=exposure to all service workers=] applies: [[CSSOM]] exposes the
    namespace to all workers, as
    <a href="https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/14229">csswg-drafts#14229</a>
    proposes.
    </div>

1.  Among [=/service workers=],
    <dfn lt="exposure to extension service workers only">expose it to
    [=extension service workers=] only</dfn>. The condition is a public
    record from the group that owns the construct. The record either
    accepts an exposure intended for [=extension service workers=] or
    declines to expose the construct to all [=/service workers=] for a
    reason the web cannot remove: the construct needs a chooser, a prompt,
    [=transient activation=], or a permission that web content cannot hold
    without a document. In either case an extension permission takes the
    place of that gate.

    The owning specification limits the `ServiceWorker` entry of the
    construct's [=exposure set=] to the [=extension service worker realm=]
    and calls the [=extension permission check=] where its algorithms would
    otherwise prompt or consult [=transient activation=]. Only the
    declaration that owns an interface can widen its [=exposure set=].

    <div class="example" id="example-declined-for-the-web">
    <a href="https://github.com/w3c/editing/issues/458">w3c/editing#458</a>
    asked the Web Editing Working Group to expose `navigator.clipboard` in
    [=/service workers=]. The group asked for web use cases and noted that
    user activation is bound to a {{Window}} and that it would not pursue
    clipboard access without a focused document and a user-visible
    affordance. The request was not taken further for the web. These
    reasons are intrinsic to the web, so
    [=exposure to extension service workers only=] applies: an extension's
    `clipboardRead` and `clipboardWrite` permissions take the place of the
    gate.
    </div>

Neither [=exposure to all service workers=] nor
[=exposure to extension service workers only=] is met by using `Worker` or
`ServiceWorker` in an [=exposure set=] to mean "[=extension service workers=]"
and narrowing the exposure in prose, in a note, or by leaving it to the
user agent.

### What the owning specification defines ### {#owning-spec-defines}

A specification that exposes a construct to [=extension service workers=]
defines the following.

: The exposure.
:: The constructs whose `ServiceWorker` exposure is limited to the
    [=extension service worker realm=], including every `partial interface`
    or member through which the construct is reached, such as a member added
    to {{WorkerNavigator}}. If the construct
    is also meant to be available in the extension's windows or dedicated
    workers, those [=global names=] appear in the same declarations.

: User-facing operations.
:: Any operation that presents a chooser, a prompt, or other user interface,
    or that requires [=transient activation=], stays `[Exposed=Window]`. If
    such an operation is needed in a worker, what replaces the user interface
    and the activation.

: Document assumptions.
:: For every algorithm step that consults [=transient activation=], the
    [=relevant global object=]'s associated {{Document}}, a
    [=browsing context=], or a top-level origin, what happens when the
    [=relevant global object=] is that of an [=extension service worker=],
    which has none of these.

: The permission.
:: Which manifest permission, host permission, or runtime-granted permission
    the [=extension permission check=] consults for the feature, in terms of
    the permission model of [[webextensions]].

: Lifetime.
:: Whether, and how, pending work keeps the worker alive and whether any
    event starts it, using {{ExtendableEvent}} and [=functional events=] from
    [[SERVICE-WORKERS]], without assuming the worker stays alive longer than
    [[SERVICE-WORKERS]] allows.

### Current exposures ### {#current-exposures}

For WebUSB and WebHID the owning specifications accepted exposure for
[=extension service workers=]
(<a href="https://github.com/whatwg/usb/pull/221">whatwg/usb#221</a>,
<a href="https://github.com/whatwg/usb/pull/224">whatwg/usb#224</a>,
<a href="https://github.com/whatwg/hid/pull/97">whatwg/hid#97</a>). For the
Clipboard API the Web Editing Working Group confirmed that it does not wish
to expose the API to all [=/service workers=]
(<a href="https://github.com/w3c/editing/issues/458">w3c/editing#458</a>),
and the exposure awaits the Web Editing Working Group attaching the
condition.

Gecko and WebKit have no construct in this category. Gecko does not ship
[=extension service workers=] by default. WebKit runs
[=extension service workers=] and injects the `browser` namespace into them
by script, but exposes no web platform interface in them beyond what the
owning specification's IDL says.

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Construct</th>
      <th>Owning specification today</th>
      <th>Chromium</th>
      <th>Gecko</th>
      <th>WebKit</th>
      <th>Intended exposure</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>{{USB}}, `WorkerNavigator.usb` and the `USB*` interfaces</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=(Worker,Window)]` [[WEBUSB]], since
          <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/usb/pull/221">whatwg/usb#221</a></td>
      <td>Windows, dedicated workers and [=extension service workers=]. Not
          shared workers, not other [=/service workers=]. `connect` and
          `disconnect` start the extension's worker.</td>
      <td>Not implemented.</td>
      <td>Not implemented.</td>
      <td>`Window` and `DedicatedWorker` unconditionally, and `ServiceWorker`
          in the [=extension service worker realm=] only. `connect` and
          `disconnect` as [=functional events=].</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{HID}}, `WorkerNavigator.hid` and the `HID*` interfaces</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=(DedicatedWorker,ServiceWorker,Window)]` with a note that
          user agents may expose it only in extension contexts [[WEBHID]],
          since <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/hid/pull/97">whatwg/hid#97</a></td>
      <td>Windows, dedicated workers and [=extension service workers=].</td>
      <td>Not implemented.</td>
      <td>Not implemented.</td>
      <td>`Window` and `DedicatedWorker` unconditionally, and `ServiceWorker`
          in the [=extension service worker realm=] only, replacing the note.
          `connect` and `disconnect` as [=functional events=].</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>{{Clipboard}}, {{ClipboardItem}} and `WorkerNavigator.clipboard`</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=Window]` only [[CLIPBOARD-APIS]]</td>
      <td>Exposed in [=extension service workers=], with no
          [=transient activation=] requirement. Writes are gated per call on
          the extension's `clipboardWrite` permission.</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=Window]`, as specified.</td>
      <td>`[Exposed=Window]`, as specified. Background clipboard access goes
          through an offscreen document.</td>
      <td>`ServiceWorker` in the [=extension service worker realm=] only, on
          {{Clipboard}}, {{ClipboardItem}} and a `WorkerNavigator` partial.
          The [=transient activation=] check replaced by the
          [=extension permission check=] for `clipboardRead` or
          `clipboardWrite` on each call. Requires a change to
          [[CLIPBOARD-APIS]].</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Issue: [[webextensions-browser-global]] declares
`partial interface ServiceWorkerGlobalScope { attribute Browser browser; }`,
which, read literally, exposes {{Browser}} in every [=/service worker=]. The
`ServiceWorker` entry of that partial's [=exposure set=] should be limited to
the [=extension service worker realm=] once WebIDL supports it.

## Security considerations ## {#security}

Exposing a construct in an [=extension service worker=] gives extension code
headless access to a capability that the web gates on one or more of a
document, a user gesture, and a prompt. The extension permission model of
[[webextensions]] takes the place of those gates: the user consents when the
permission is granted, at install time or at run time, and can withdraw it.

Where an algorithm of the owning specification would prompt, consult
[=transient activation=], or evaluate a document's permissions policy, in an
[=extension service worker=] it instead runs the
[=extension permission check=] for the feature. A prompt is never shown from
an [=extension service worker=], and [[PERMISSIONS-POLICY]], which defines no
policy for a [=/service worker=]'s settings object, is not consulted.

The user agent must run the [=extension permission check=] on each use of the
capability, so that a permission revoked while the worker is running takes
effect on the next use.

The user agent must make use of such a capability from an
[=extension service worker=] attributable to the extension in its user
interface, since the tab-level indicators that accompany the capability in a
window are not available.

Whether an [=extension service worker=] runs in a private browsing session,
and with which granted permissions, is governed by [[webextensions]].

## Privacy considerations ## {#privacy}

This specification exposes nothing to web content. An
[=extension service worker realm=] belongs only to an
[=extension service worker=], whose script already runs with the extension's
permissions. What the headless path removes is the user
gesture and the visible surface that accompany the same capability in a
window, which is why [[#security]] requires use to be attributable to the
extension.

